.TH CHTTPCLIENT_SET_INACTIVITY_TIMEOUT 3 "2026-10-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_set_inactivity_timeout \- set how long a request may wait on a silent connection
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_set_inactivity_timeout(chttpcli cli,
                                                 uint64_t us);

#define CHTTPCLI_DEFAULT_INACTIVITY_TIMEOUT_US ((uint64_t)60000000)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function sets how long a request may wait on its connection with
no byte sent or received. When it has waited that long, the request
ends with ccol_timed_out. 0 turns the timeout off. The default is
CHTTPCLI_DEFAULT_INACTIVITY_TIMEOUT_US, 60 s.

Every byte that moves in either direction starts the count again. A
transfer that keeps moving therefore never reaches this timeout,
however slowly it moves and however long it takes in total; the
request timeout of chttpclient_set_request_timeout(3) is the limit for
the whole request.

Only time spent waiting on the connection counts. The time that the
write_fn of a streaming request takes, between two reads, is not
counted.

The count starts once the connection is up, since the connect timeout
of chttpclient_set_connect_timeout(3) bounds the connect and the TLS
handshake. It covers the sending of the request, the wait for the
first byte of the response and the reading of the response. A server
that takes longer than this timeout to start its response, such as one
that holds a long poll open, needs a larger value or 0.

chttpclient_do, chttpclient_do_async, chttpclient_do_pooled and their
streaming forms apply it on every hop of a request. For
chttpclient_do_async and chttpclient_do_pooled an engine thread
watches it, and a request ends at most about 100 ms after its
connection has been silent for the timeout.

Any value other than 0 is honoured as a limit however far away it is:
a value too large for the clock to reach saturates to the latest
deadline the clock can hold.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli   The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_NULL.
us    The timeout in microseconds. 0 means no timeout.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success      The client has the new timeout.
ccol_invalid_args cli is CHTTPCLI_NULL, or a stale handle of a
                  client that has been destroyed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpclient_set_connect_timeout (3),
.BR chttpclient_set_request_timeout (3),
.BR chttpclient_do (3),
.BR chttpclient (7)
